<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="AZD: Edit Azure Pipelines YAML" id="Azd-Pipeline-Yaml">

    <primary-label ref="azd"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Configure: <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Pipeline Settings</ui-path></p>
        <p>Language server: requires Node.js 14 or later</p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Get completion, validation and hover help in Azure Pipelines YAML files, and choose which files count as pipelines.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Completion and validation for Azure Pipelines YAML through the Azure Pipelines language server.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Edit Azure Pipelines YAML in JetBrains IDEs with AZD: completion, validation and hover help from the
        Azure Pipelines language server, and custom file locations.</web-summary>

    <p>
        AZD recognizes Azure Pipelines YAML files and adds completion, validation and hover documentation to them.
        Pipeline files get the Azure Pipelines icon in the <control>Project</control> tool window.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Completion and validation" id="yaml-completion">
        <p>
            When you open a pipeline file, AZD starts the Azure Pipelines language server that ships with the plugin.
            The language server provides completion of keys such as <code>stages</code>, <code>jobs</code> and <code>steps</code>,
            hover documentation, and diagnostics for unknown or misplaced keys.
        </p>
        <img src="pipeline_injections.png" alt="Completion popup in a pipeline YAML file suggesting stages, steps and strategy"
             width="706" thumbnail="true" border-effect="rounded"/>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_yaml_completion.png" alt="Completion popup in azure-pipelines.yml suggesting stages, steps and strategy" replaces="pipeline_injections.png" -->
        <p>
            The language server runs on Node.js 14 or later.
            If AZD cannot find Node.js, pipeline files get no completion; see <a anchor="yaml-nodejs"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
            To turn the language server off, clear <control>Inject Azure DevOps YAML pipeline schema</control> in
            <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Pipeline Settings</ui-path>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Which files are pipeline files" id="pipeline-files">
        <p>AZD treats a <path>.yml</path> or <path>.yaml</path> file as a pipeline file when:</p>
        <list>
            <li>its name contains <code>azure</code> or <code>pipelines</code>, for example <path>azure-pipelines.yml</path> or <path>ci-pipelines.yaml</path>;</li>
            <li>it is inside a folder named <path>azure-pipelines</path>, <path>.azure-pipelines</path> or <path>.pipelines</path>;</li>
            <li>it matches an entry in <control>Additional pipeline YAML locations</control>.</li>
        </list>

        <procedure title="Add pipeline file locations" id="add-yaml-locations">
            <step>
                <p>Open <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Pipeline Settings</ui-path>.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>In <control>Additional pipeline YAML locations</control>, enter folders or glob patterns relative to the project root, separated by semicolons.
                    For example: <code>ci/pipelines;deploy/**/pipeline-*.yml</code>.</p>
                <p>A folder makes every YAML file under it a pipeline file.
                    A glob pattern matches specific files.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Click <control>OK</control>.
                    The <control>Project</control> tool window updates the file icons.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>This setting belongs to the current project.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Set up Node.js for the language server" id="yaml-nodejs">
        <p>
            AZD uses the first Node.js 14 or later that it finds, in this order: the path in <control>Node.js Path</control>,
            the Node.js interpreter configured in the IDE (in IDEs with JavaScript support), the <code>PATH</code>,
            and on macOS and Linux, nvm installations and your login shell.
            If it is not found, or you want to use another installation, set the path yourself.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Configure Node.js" id="configure-nodejs">
            <step>
                <p>Open <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Pipeline Settings</ui-path>.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>Node.js Configuration</control> group, enter the Node.js executable in <control>Node.js Path</control>,
                    or leave it empty to detect Node.js automatically.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Click <control>Validate Node.js</control> and check the status line below the field.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Open the YAML of a run" id="jump-to-yaml">
        <p>
            To open the pipeline definition file from a run, use <control>Jump to YAML</control>; see
            <a href="Azure-DevOps-Pipelines.topic" anchor="run-details">Inspect a run</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

